\section{Projects}{

A \verb{_targets.yaml} can define several projects, each with its own script
and store, and the \code{TAR_PROJECT} environment variable chooses one
(\code{"main"} unless set): see the targets manual's chapter on projects. The
board starts on that project, or on \code{project}, and a Project field left of
the Script field switches it, when \verb{_targets.yaml} has more than one
project or the board isn't on \code{"main"}. Loading another project's script
switches to that project too. The board's project is its own: your R
session's \code{TAR_PROJECT} is never changed, and the Script tab gives the
line to make it follow.
}

\section{Large pipelines}{

The page keeps every target on the graph in memory, so very large graphs
can make the browser slow or crash the tab. When a script loaded from the
Script field has more than 500 targets on its graph, the board asks
before drawing it, and Cancel keeps the current graph. Set the limit with
\code{options(targetsboard.confirm_nodes = )} before starting the board, e.g.
\code{Inf} never to ask.
}

\section{Runs}{

Run uses \code{\link[targets:tar_make]{targets::tar_make()}}, unless the script sets up future
(\code{future::plan()}) or clustermq (\code{options(clustermq.scheduler = )}): then
\code{\link[targets:tar_make_future]{targets::tar_make_future()}} or \code{\link[targets:tar_make_clustermq]{targets::tar_make_clustermq()}}, and the
run summary says which. Their workers are a literal \verb{workers =} in the
\code{plan()} call, or 1; set them with \code{options(targetsboard.workers = )}.
The script is read for these calls, never run.
}

\section{Your editor}{

Started from RStudio, Positron (with the rstudioapi package) or VS Code's
terminal (with its \code{code} command), a target's right-click menu has "Open
in editor": its \verb{tar_*()} call, or a function's definition, in the script
or the \verb{R/} folder, opened at its line. The board runs in the background,
so it asks this session to open it: this works while the session is idle
at its prompt (it needs the later package). Without an editor, the menu
has no such item.
}

\section{Value previews}{

A double-click on a target opens its value in a frame, with a tab per view
of it and a last tab with what \code{print()} shows. A class gets its own views
with a \code{\link[=tar_board_preview]{tar_board_preview()}} method, from a package or from the project's
\verb{_targetsboard.R}. A value larger than 100 MB in the store is read only
when asked; set the limit with \code{options(targetsboard.preview_bytes = )}.
}

\section{Themes}{

The board's look is the "Targets board" theme. A few Bootstrap themes
from Bootswatch, checked for readable text in light and dark mode, can
replace it: pick one in the top bar, and the browser remembers it. A dark
theme, such as Darkly, keeps the page in dark mode and hides the switch.
To start the board with a theme, whatever the browser remembers, set
\code{options(targetsboard.theme = )} to its name, e.g. \code{"Cosmo"}.
}

